1
00:00:00,319 --> 00:00:01,799
window.

2
00:00:01,799 --> 00:00:06,719
Too late, someone thought of racing ahead and shutting the five-barred gate.

3
00:00:06,719 --> 00:00:12,680
But in another moment, the van was through it and rapidly disappearing down the road.

4
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:16,959
Boxer was never seen again.

5
00:00:16,959 --> 00:00:22,120
Three days later, it was announced that he had died in the hospital at Willingdon

6
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:26,959
in spite of receiving every attention a horse could have.

7
00:00:26,959 --> 00:00:30,360
Squealer came to announce the news to the others.

8
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:35,280
He had, he said, been present during Boxer's last hours.

9
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:41,479
>> It was the most affecting sight I have ever seen, >> said Squealer, lifting his trotter and

10
00:00:41,479 --> 00:00:43,399
wiping away a tear.

11
00:00:43,399 --> 00:00:48,120
>> I was at his bedside at the very last, and at the end, almost too weak to

12
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:53,479
speak, he whispered in my ear that his sole sorrow was to have passed on before

13
00:00:53,479 --> 00:00:55,479
the windmill was finished.

14
00:00:55,479 --> 00:00:58,239
"Forward, comrades," he whispered.

15
00:00:58,239 --> 00:01:01,239
"Forward in the name of the rebellion.

16
00:01:01,239 --> 00:01:09,959
Long live Animal Farm. Long live Comrade Napoleon. Napoleon is always right."

17
00:01:09,959 --> 00:01:13,680
Those were his very last words, comrades.

18
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:18,520
>> Here, Squealer's demeanor suddenly changed. He fell silent for a moment,

19
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:24,120
and his little eyes darted suspicious glances from side to side before he proceeded.

20
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:28,760
It had come to his knowledge, he said, that a foolish and wicked rumor had been

21
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:31,879
circulated at the time of Boxer's removal.

22
00:01:31,879 --> 00:01:37,360
Some of the animals had noticed that the van which took Boxer away was marked

23
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:43,920
"horse slaughterer" and had actually jumped to the conclusion that Boxer was being sent to

24
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:45,840
the knackers.

25
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:52,440
It was almost unbelievable, said Squealer, that any animal could be so stupid.

26
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:58,399
"Surely," he cried indignantly, whisking his tail and skipping from side to side,

27
00:01:58,399 --> 00:02:04,719
"surely they knew their beloved leader, Comrade Napoleon, better than that."

28
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:08,040
But the explanation was really very simple.

29
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:13,479
The van had previously been the property of the and had been bought by the veterinary

30
00:02:13,479 --> 00:02:17,559
surgeon who had not yet painted the old name out.

31
00:02:17,559 --> 00:02:20,440
That was how the mistake had arisen.

32
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:25,280
The animals were enormously relieved to hear this and when Squealer went on to

33
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:30,440
give further graphic details of Boxer's deathbed, the admirable care he had

34
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:34,959
received and the expensive medicines for which Napoleon had paid without a

35
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:41,440
thought as to the cost, their last doubts disappeared and the sorrow that they felt for their

36
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:47,879
comrade's death was tempered by the thought that at least he had died happy.

37
00:02:47,879 --> 00:02:52,040
Napoleon himself appeared at the meeting on the following Sunday morning and

38
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:56,200
pronounced a short oration in Boxer's honor.

39
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,600
It had not been possible, he said, to bring back their lamented comrade's

40
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:05,840
remains for interment on the farm, but he had ordered a large wreath to be

41
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:12,799
made from the laurels in the farmhouse garden and sent down to be placed on Boxer's grave.

42
00:03:12,799 --> 00:03:20,479
And in a few days time, the pigs intended to hold a memorial banquet in Boxer's honor.

43
00:03:20,479 --> 00:03:25,000
Napoleon ended his speech with a reminder of Boxer's two favorite maxims.
